Lab 6: Continuous Monitoring and Logging with ELK Stack
Objective
Set up the ELK (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana) stack locally and simulate sending logs from a deep learning model to visualize them in Kibana. This enables real-time monitoring of model performance and behavior — without using the cloud.

Prerequisites
Start your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine

Ensure the following tools are installed:

Java (required by Elasticsearch & Logstash)

ELK Stack (Elasticsearch, Logstash, Kibana)

Install Java:
sudo apt update
sudo apt install default-jre -y
Download and Install ELK Stack:
You can download .deb packages from https://www.elastic.co/downloads and install with sudo dpkg -i <package>.deb. Below is a local setup method using APT:

wget -qO - https://artifacts.elastic.co/GPG-KEY-elasticsearch | sudo apt-key add -
sudo apt install apt-transport-https
echo "deb https://artifacts.elastic.co/packages/7.x/apt stable main" | sudo tee /etc/apt/sources.list.d/elastic-7.x.list
sudo apt update
sudo apt install elasticsearch kibana logstash -y
Steps
Step 1: Start ELK Services
Start services in separate terminals or as background processes:

sudo systemctl start elasticsearch
sudo systemctl enable elasticsearch

sudo systemctl start kibana
sudo systemctl enable kibana

sudo systemctl start logstash
sudo systemctl enable logstash
Visit Kibana in your browser:
http://localhost:5601

Step 2: Simulate Model Log Generation
Create a folder for simulated logs:

mkdir -p ~/elk-lab/logs
cd ~/elk-lab/logs
Create a mock log file:

cat > model_log.json <<EOF
{"timestamp": "2025-07-09T12:00:00Z", "model": "iris", "accuracy": 0.95, "latency_ms": 123}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-09T12:05:00Z", "model": "iris", "accuracy": 0.94, "latency_ms": 118}
{"timestamp": "2025-07-09T12:10:00Z", "model": "iris", "accuracy": 0.96, "latency_ms": 110}
EOF
Continuously append to this log:

watch -n 10 "echo '{\"timestamp\": \"$(date -Iseconds)\", \"model\": \"iris\", \"accuracy\": 0.9, \"latency_ms\": $((100 + RANDOM % 30))}' >> model_log.json"
Step 3: Configure Logstash to Ingest Logs
Create Logstash config:

sudo nano /etc/logstash/conf.d/model-log.conf
Paste this config:

input {
  file {
    path => "/home/ubuntu/elk-lab/logs/model_log.json"
    start_position => "beginning"
    sincedb_path => "/dev/null"
    codec => "json"
  }
}

filter {
  date {
    match => ["timestamp", "ISO8601"]
  }
}

output {
  elasticsearch {
    hosts => ["localhost:9200"]
    index => "model-logs"
  }
  stdout { codec => rubydebug }
}
Restart Logstash:

sudo systemctl restart logstash
Step 4: Visualize in Kibana
Open Kibana at: http://localhost:5601

Go to "Discover" → Create Index Pattern → Enter model-logs

Choose timestamp as the time field

Start exploring logs and create visualizations in "Visualize" → e.g., line chart of accuracy over time

Conclusion
In this lab, you:

Installed and configured the ELK stack locally

Simulated real-time logging from a deep learning model

Used Kibana to visualize model performance metrics like accuracy and latency

This gives you practical insight into continuous monitoring and observability of AI systems — completely offline on your Al Nafi-provided Ubuntu machine.
